TY  - JOUR
AU  - LAFRANCE, WILLIAM
TI  - NEUROIMAGING BIOMARKER FOR SEIZURES
M3  - Awarded Grant
AB  - Background:  Veterans with post-traumatic epilepsy (PTE) or with psychogenic non-epileptic seizures (PNES) are often severely disabled, difficult to treat, and are frequently encountered in neurology, psychiatry, and emergency departments.  Epileptic seizures and PNES have the same appearance clinically, but have significantly different etiologies and treatments.  PTE is caused by abnormal brain cell discharges.  PNES is a conversion disorder caused by trauma and stress and manifests neurologically with seizure-like episodes.  Both types of seizures are common and disabling among Veterans and civilians.  Up to 400,000 people in the U.S. have PNES.  A quarter of the 80,000 Veterans with seizures have PNES.  Many people with PNES are misdiagnosed with epilepsy and undergo invasive procedures and are prescribed anti-epileptic drugs (AEDs), which do not treat PNES.Even though PNES have been described in the medical literature for millennia, effective treatments are limited.  The National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (NINDS) Curing Epilepsy Benchmarks include developing treatments for PNES.  We have developed and validated an innovative treatment, cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) for PNES, which showed a significant reduction in PNES in our pilot studies, which is based on CBT for seizures.  Understanding brain neurophysiology using neuroimaging in patients with seizure disorders will provide treatment targets for PNES/PTE.  This study will examine Veterans with PTE and PNES and will compare them to Veterans with traumatic brain injury (TBI) who do not have seizures.Hypothesis 1:  We hypothesize, based on our preliminary data, that baseline functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) in all groups will show aberrant responses to stress and abnormal emotional processing between those with seizures vs. those without seizure disorders, with differences predominantly observed in the medial frontal cortices and amygdala with these differences being most abnormal in the PNES group.Specific Aim 1:  To compare neural correlates of response to emotional and stressful stimuli in 88 Veterans with PNES (44/site) and 88 Veterans with PTE (44/site) before and after cognitive challenge (CBT-Sz).  Veterans with PNES or PTE will be compared to 88 Veterans with a history of TBI who do not have PTE/PNES in order to control for common comorbidities including depression, anxiety, and PTSD and to show fMRI signal stability between and within the individuals.Hypothesis 2:  We hypothesize that CBT-Sz will lead to changes in the fMRI signals related to stress probes and in amygdala connectivity as shown through diffusion tensor imaging (DTI) and fMRI.  This change will, in turn, be predicted by the magnitude of clinical responses defined by seizure frequency improvement over time and secondarily by targeted behavioral measures.Specific Aim 2:  To investigate changes in neural circuitry for emotional processing and stress response in the Veterans after receiving CBT-Sz and to correlate these changes with symptomatic improvement.Ours will be the first study to investigate mechanisms in neural response to emotion and stress fMRI probes in Veterans with PNES and PTE and how they compare to those in Veterans with a traumatic brain injury history without PNES or PTE.  By utilizing the approach aiming at modifying, with behavioral intervention (CBT-Sz), the cognitive/psychological status of participants, we will convert the predominantly theoretically and empirically based approach to behavioral therapy (CBT-Sz) into an approach that is based on understanding of the mechanisms by which CBT exerts its effects on the brain and its plasticity.Research Strategy:  In this proposed two-site study, we will enroll 88 patients with video-EEG documented PNES and 88 with confirmed epilepsy and will obtain functional neuroimaging before and after they receive a behavioral challenge using the CBT-Seizures.  We will compare the neuroimaging characteristics of response to emotion and stress probes in 176 patients to those of 88 controls with TBI without seizures to test the hypothesis that patients faulty processing of emotions and stress in patients with PNES or PTE and aberrant brain connectivity differ between the groups and that these characteristics can be altered in patients with seizures by the behavioral challenge (CBT-Sz).  This is the first study investigating abnormalities in the mechanisms of emotion and stress processing in both PNES and PTE.  Results will provide increased understanding of neural circuitry associated with these disorders and will inform new treatment development.  We anticipate this new knowledge can change clinical neurologic and psychiatric practices for PNES.
