TI  - Patient tissues differentiating method for            diagnosing e.g. epilepsy, involves applying pre-pulse            to suppress signals from water bound to macromolecules,            and computing values representing macromolecular            content of tissues
AB  - 
                   NOVELTY - The method involves applying a magnetization                transfer (MT) pre-pulse in combination with a                diffusion weighted imaging (DWI) pulse sequence to                produce an image of a tissue under evaluation.                Analysis maps or measurements are generated from                the produced image. Values representative of                macromolecular content of the tissues are computed                from the map, where the pre-pulse suppresses                signals from water bound to macromolecules.
                    USE - Used for differentiating tissues in magnetic                resonance imaging for diagnosing cystic tumour,                aspirated abscess, and psychiatric disorder e.g.                conversion disorder, schizophrenia, unipolar or                bipolar depression and epilepsy, on a                patient.
                    ADVANTAGE - The method computes the values representing                the macromolecular content of the tissues, thus                enabling evaluation of the combined effects of                diffusion and magnetization transfer on normal                brain parenchyma and pathological tissues. The                method thus obtains quantifiable differentiation of                the tissues, and hence improving the tissue                specificity in pathologies related to variable                macromolecular size.
                    DESCRIPTION Of DRAWING(S) - The drawing shows a pulse sequence in a                tissue differentiating method.
