TY  - JOUR
AU  - MUELLER, SUSANNE
TI  - THE IMPRINT OF PSYCHOGENIC NON-EPILEPTIC SEIZURES ON THE BRAIN: A NEW MODEL AND IMAGING BIOMARKER
M3  - Awarded Grant
AB  - Background:  Psychogenic non-epileptic seizures (PNES) are defined by the occurrence of seizure-like episodes that interfere with normal functioning but lack the characteristic ictal electroencephalogram (EEG) manifestations of epileptic seizures.  Up to 20% of the patients who have initially been diagnosed with epilepsy ultimately receive the diagnosis of PNES, making this disorder as common as multiple sclerosis or Parkinson’s disease.  PNES are thought to be mediated by psychological stress factors, but the biological mechanisms underlying it are currently unknown.Hypothesis:  The overall goal of this application is to confirm a new PNES mechanism by identifying its characteristic signature in task-free functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) data of PNES patients and demonstrating a relationship between PNES severity and the expression of this signature.  The new mechanism assumes that PNES is associated with a predisposition for an overshooting recruitment.  Overshooting recruitment describes a state that is characterized by an enhanced synchronization between brain regions normally involved in emotion control (prefrontal-limbic regions) and by the additional recruitment of regions involved in abnormal emotion processing (cortico-basal ganglia circuitry, parietal, temporal regions).  Repeated or prolonged stress or traumatic experiences further reinforces this predisposition, which renders the brain more susceptible for overshooting recruitment and leaves a characteristic imprint that is detectable in the individual’s task-free fMRI even in the absence of stress.  On the behavioral level, overshooting allows for aspects of pathological emotion processing, e.g., anxiety, to become apparent during mild stress and facilitates overshooting reactions severe enough to recruit the additional brain regions required to generate the individual’s typical PNES, e.g., precentral, striatal regions, in moderate to high stress situations.  Previous task-free fMRI studies using traditional group level processing methods that calculate temporal correlations between brain regions over the whole acquisition time found supporting evidence for the hypothesized overshooting in form of increased functional connectivity in prefronto-limbic regions.  However, the transient imprint of each individual’s pathologic network that is the hallmark of the proposed PNES model and reflects best the severity and the individual clinical features of a patient’s PNES will be missed by this approach.  Recently, several strategies for dynamic resting state fMRI analysis have been proposed.  A common strategy is the sliding windows approach that will be used in this project together with graph analysis and hierarchical cluster analysis to identify the characteristic signature of the hypothesized phases of overshooting.Specific Aim 1:  PNES is associated with phases of overshooting recruitment between regions involved in emotion processing whose configuration reflects aspects of the individual’s pathological emotion processing and PNES semiology.  The purpose of this aim is to demonstrate that the new dynamic approach is capable to capture the hypothesized overshooting signature at the group level and in individual subjects.  (A) The total duration during which the overshooting signature is present in a subject is longer in PNES than in controls and is positively correlated with the psychiatric comorbidity score and seizure frequency.  (B) The network configurations (modularity based connectivity patterns) observed during these phases are compatible with abnormal emotion processing (increased interaction within prefrontal-limbic regions expanding to thalamus, striatum, temporal, and parietal regions) and reflect the PNES semiology.Specific Aim 2:  PNES is characterized by increased stationary connectivity in prefrontal-limbic regions involved in emotion control.  The purpose of this aim is to establish a link with traditional stationary analysis methods and to demonstrate that the stationary analysis provides an incomplete description of the overshooting recruitment at the group level.  (A) PNES patients as a group are characterized by increased global (averaged over all regions) and regional (regions involved in emotional control) functional connectivity in the compared to controls, but the increased functional connectivity does not reach significance in regions involved in pathological emotion processing.  (B) Increased functional connectivity in prefrontal limbic but not cortico-basal ganglia circuitry, etc. is positively correlated with the comorbidity score and seizure frequency, but this correlation is weaker than in the dynamic analysis.  (C) The stationary network configuration does not reflect the seizure semiology at the individual level as well as the dynamic network configuration.Research Strategy:  The project is designed as a cross-sectional study and will enroll 40 PNES patients and 20 controls.  All will undergo fMRI on a 3T magnet and a standardized assessment regarding PNES risk factors and psychiatric comorbidities that will be used to calculate comorbidity scores.  To reduce the number of potential signatures different types of synchronization signals, e.g., weak vs. strong, etc. will be simulated in synthetic data and their graph analytical signature characterized.  Identifying one of simulated signatures in the real data will not only provide support that this signature indeed corresponds to the hypothesized enhanced synchronization/overshooting recruitment but also provide insight into its nature.
